    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/kingweb/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/chris-1-1.jpeg"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/new-events/the-best-of-boxwood/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "THE BEST OF BOXWOOD"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "<p>Each year, Boxwood Festival culminates in a ‘Best of Boxwood’ tour across Nova Scotia presented by Musique Royale. 2025 marks Boxwood’s 25th anniversary, and Musique Royale’s 40th Summer Festival! This year’s ‘Best of Boxwood’ tour features Brid Harper (one of Ireland’s leading traditional fiddle players for over forty years), Colin Cotter (Californian guitarist and singer/songwriter), David Greenberg (Cape Breton fiddler and baroque violinist), and Chris Norman (pipes and flutes). “Best of Boxwood” brings the energetic, traditional dance music of<br /> the Maritimes, Scotland, Ireland and more to King&#8217;s Theatre.</p> <p><strong>About the Artists:</strong></p> <p><strong>Bríd Harper</strong> is regarded as one of the leading Irish traditional fiddle players of the last forty years. She grew up close to the east Donegal village of Castlefinn and started playing the fiddle at the age of nine. There is music on both sides of her family. In the mid 1970’s, Bríd won all the underage competitions at the annual All Ireland Fleadh Cheoil both in the Slow Air and Dance Music categories. In 1988 she won three prestigious titles &#8211; Senior All Ireland, Fiddler of Dooney and Oireachtas na Gaeilge. She is a visiting tutor and performer at The Irish World Academy in Limerick University. Her much anticipated solo recording “Bríd Harper” released in 2015 was described as “solo fiddle of the most exquisite kind” and “ one of this year’s best traditional albums” (Tradconnect.com).</p> <p><strong>David Greenberg</strong> has enjoyed a double career as a Baroque violinist and Cape Breton fiddler for over three decades. His fluency and experience in these two genres makes him uniquely qualified to interpret the wild music of 18th-century Scotland. David is a graduate of Indiana University’s Early Music Institute, where he studied with Stanley Ritchie. He has performed, taught, and recorded primarily in North America and Western Europe, as well as in Australia, and New Zealand.David has performed with Tafelmusik and as guest soloist/director with several orchestras, including the Calgary Symphony Orchestra and Symphony Nova Scotia.</p> <p><strong>Colin Cotter</strong> is a multi-instrumentalist from the San Francisco Bay Area. When he was four years old, his mom gave him a fiddle and began teaching him to play the tunes she had learned herself as a child, building on several generations of a family tradition in Finnish folk music. He fell in love with the Scottish and Irish traditional tunes and songs that would come to play a lead role in his ongoing musical journey after attending a fiddle camp at a young age. He has played gigs from coast to coast in the USA, toured internationally in Canada, Ireland, and Spain, and teaches regularly at some of the top folk music camps in the US.</p> <p><strong>Chris Norman</strong> was born in Halifax, Nova Scotia and developed an early passion for traditional Maritime, Celtic and French Canadian music. As a performer and composer, he is a champion and standard-bearer for these forms while simultaneously exploring their boundaries and fusing them with classical styles. In recognition of his significant, unique and wide-ranging achievements he was presented with the Queen Elizabeth II Diamond Jubilee Medal for contributions to the arts in Canada in 2012, and awarded the degree of Doctor of Laws, honoris causa by Dalhousie University in 2015.</p> <p><em>Chris Norman is &#8221; . . a flute player of spectacular and imaginative virtuosity” &#8212; The <cite>New Yorker</cite></em></p> <p><strong>Musique Royale</strong> is a province-wide music presenter in Nova Scotia. Since its founding in 1985, Musique Royale has brought world class music to venues of architectural and historic significance across the province. Hear symphony orchestras and chorus’ in beautiful churches and salon-style events, with children-friendly shows encompassing jazz, world, classical, and more. From June to September our Summer Festival features early music in beautiful and historic venues across Nova Scotia. Whatever your appetite for music is, Musique Royale has something for everyone!</p> <div> <p><strong>TICKETS: </strong></p> <ul> <li><strong>$30 Adults</strong></li> <li><strong>$20 Supported*</strong></li> <li><strong>$5 Youth</strong></li> </ul> <div class='style__notes___25v7J'> <div class='main-title'> <div class='TitleBlock__TitleMetaDataContainer-sc-1nlhx7j-2 hWHMKr'> <p><em>HST additional</em></p> <p><em>*Supported tickets are a discounted rate for those who need it, made possible thanks to our generous donors.</em></p> <p>King’s Theatre is committed to making live performances accessible to all audiences. If cost is a barrier please contact our Box Office for Bursary Tickets: boxoffice@kingstheatre.ca or 902-532-7704.</p> <p><strong>Doors open 45 minutes before showtime.<br /> </strong></p> <p><small><a href='https://www.kingstheatre.ca/about/ticket-info/'><b>Click here for King’s Ticket policies</b></a></small></p> </div> </div> </div> </div> <div></div> <div> <p><iframe title='Telemann&#039;s Vivace from Duo in E' width='1080' height='608' src='https://www.youtube.com/embed/Xg02qSuwYX8?feature=oembed'  allow='accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share' referrerpolicy='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' allowfullscreen></iframe></p> </div> <div></div> <div></div> <div> <p><iframe title='Chris Norman: The Iron Ring (Wooden Flute - Boxwood Festival 2008)' width='1080' height='810' src='https://www.youtube.com/embed/aVl-0VkrbHc?feature=oembed'  allow='accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share' referrerpolicy='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' allowfullscreen></iframe></p> <p><a href='http://isu.pub/j3KwVwL'><img class='wp-image-241938 aligncenter' src='https://www.kingstheatre.ca/kingweb/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Screenshot-2025-07-25-at-12.10.39-PM-212x300.png' alt='' width='418' height='592' /></a></p> </div> <p>&nbsp;</p> "
      }
    ],
