    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "THE BEST OF BOXWOOD"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n                Each year, Boxwood Festival culminates in a ‘Best of Boxwood’ tour across Nova Scotia presented by Musique Royale. 2025 marks Boxwood’s 25th anniversary, and Musique Royale’s 40th Summer Festival! This year’s ‘Best of Boxwood’ tour features Brid Harper (one of Ireland’s leading traditional fiddle players for over forty years), Colin Cotter (Californian guitarist and singer/songwriter), David Greenberg (Cape Breton fiddler and baroque violinist), and Chris Norman (pipes and flutes). “Best of Boxwood” brings the energetic, traditional dance music of\nthe Maritimes, Scotland, Ireland and more to King’s Theatre.\nAbout the Artists:\nBríd Harper is regarded as one of the leading Irish traditional fiddle players of the last forty years. She grew up close to the east Donegal village of Castlefinn and started playing the fiddle at the age of nine. There is music on both sides of her family. In the mid 1970’s, Bríd won all the underage competitions at the annual All Ireland Fleadh Cheoil both in the Slow Air and Dance Music categories. In 1988 she won three prestigious titles – Senior All Ireland, Fiddler of Dooney and Oireachtas na Gaeilge. She is a visiting tutor and performer at The Irish World Academy in Limerick University. Her much anticipated solo recording “Bríd Harper” released in 2015 was described as “solo fiddle of the most exquisite kind” and “ one of this year’s best traditional albums” (Tradconnect.com).\nDavid Greenberg has enjoyed a double career as a Baroque violinist and Cape Breton fiddler for over three decades. His fluency and experience in these two genres makes him uniquely qualified to interpret the wild music of 18th-century Scotland. David is a graduate of Indiana University’s Early Music Institute, where he studied with Stanley Ritchie. He has performed, taught, and recorded primarily in North America and Western Europe, as well as in Australia, and New Zealand.David has performed with Tafelmusik and as guest soloist/director with several orchestras, including the Calgary Symphony Orchestra and Symphony Nova Scotia.\nColin Cotter is a multi-instrumentalist from the San Francisco Bay Area. When he was four years old, his mom gave him a fiddle and began teaching him to play the tunes she had learned herself as a child, building on several generations of a family tradition in Finnish folk music. He fell in love with the Scottish and Irish traditional tunes and songs that would come to play a lead role in his ongoing musical journey after attending a fiddle camp at a young age. He has played gigs from coast to coast in the USA, toured internationally in Canada, Ireland, and Spain, and teaches regularly at some of the top folk music camps in the US.\nChris Norman was born in Halifax, Nova Scotia and developed an early passion for traditional Maritime, Celtic and French Canadian music. As a performer and composer, he is a champion and standard-bearer for these forms while simultaneously exploring their boundaries and fusing them with classical styles. In recognition of his significant, unique and wide-ranging achievements he was presented with the Queen Elizabeth II Diamond Jubilee Medal for contributions to the arts in Canada in 2012, and awarded the degree of Doctor of Laws, honoris causa by Dalhousie University in 2015.\nChris Norman is ” . . a flute player of spectacular and imaginative virtuosity” — The New Yorker\nMusique Royale is a province-wide music presenter in Nova Scotia. Since its founding in 1985, Musique Royale has brought world class music to venues of architectural and historic significance across the province. Hear symphony orchestras and chorus’ in beautiful churches and salon-style events, with children-friendly shows encompassing jazz, world, classical, and more. From June to September our Summer Festival features early music in beautiful and historic venues across Nova Scotia. Whatever your appetite for music is, Musique Royale has something for everyone!\n\nTICKETS: \n\n$30 Adults\n$20 Supported*\n$5 Youth\n\n\n\n\nHST additional\n*Supported tickets are a discounted rate for those who need it, made possible thanks to our generous donors.\nKing’s Theatre is committed to making live performances accessible to all audiences. If cost is a barrier please contact our Box Office for Bursary Tickets: boxoffice@kingstheatre.ca or 902-532-7704.\nDoors open 45 minutes before showtime.\n\nClick here for King’s Ticket policies\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n            "
      }
