  {
    "@id": "https://kingstheatre.ca/events/event_242421_0",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Event"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/new-events/the-nazih-borish-trio/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/kingweb/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/nazih-2.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://kingstheatre.ca/events/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "THE NAZIH BORISH TRIO"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "<p><strong>The Nazih Borish Trio</strong></p> <p>Nazih Borish is a Montreal-based oud player and composer from Syria who is recognized throughout the Arab world and beyond as one the world’s foremost experts of the oud. Joined by well-establish Canadian jazz and classical bassist Roberto Occhipinti and the versatile world percussionist Nick Halley, the Trio performs original compositions of new music inspired by the various music traditions of classical Arabic, Syrian, flamenco and jazz.</p> <p><strong>Nazih Borish</strong> was born in Lattakia, Syria and began teaching himself oud at the age of five. In addition to immersing himself in the Arab tradition, Nazih’s unique musical style has been influenced by various musical traditions including Jazz, Blues, Flamenco, and Indian. At the age of thirteen, Nazih began preforming professionally across Syria and the Arab world with many artists. His experience performing professionally at a young age, and his many musical influences have established Nazih as one of the most innovative oudists of his time, pushing the instrument beyond its music limits.</p> <p>In 2005, Nazih founded the Syrian Oud School in his hometown of Lattakia, continuing his work as a performer and teacher until he moved to Montreal, Canada in 2016. Nazih has recently performed with such ensembles as Constantinople (Quebec), the Canadian Arabic Orchestra (Ontario) and the Rouh Trio (Jordan), and has taken part in artistic residencies at the Lunenburg Academy of Music Performance (Culturally INappropriate, 2018, 2019) and the Centre des Musiciens du Monde (Nazih Borish’s Log, 2018). He is regularly invited to collaborate with artists in Europe, North Africa and the Middle East, and has served as a judge and special guest at international oud competitions.</p> <p><strong>Roberto Occhipinti</strong> is a Canadian jazz bassist, composer, and music producer. Born in Toronto to a prominent musical family, he studied music at the University of Toronto under his mentors, Joel Quarrington and Dave Young. Occhipinti is a versatile musician who has performed in classical orchestras, jazz ensembles, Latin groups, rhythm and blues outfits, and rock bands. He has worked in theatre and opera, written for radio and television, played on film soundtracks, and appeared on numerous records.</p> <p>Occhipinti was nominated for a Juno Award for Contemporary Jazz Album of the Year two times, first at the Juno Awards of 2006 for his album Yemaya and later at the Juno Awards of 2009 for A Bend in the River. He often collaborates with his younger brother Michael Occhipinti in the Neufeld-Occhipinti Jazz Orchestra and Creation Dream, and has performed as a member of the Winnipeg Symphony Orchestra, the Hamilton Symphony Orchestra and the Canadian Opera Company. He is always delighted to join his friends and musical collaborators, Nick Halley and Nazih Borish.</p> <p><strong>Nick Halley</strong> is a drummer/percussionist, keyboardist, composer, and conductor based in Nova Scotia, Canada. Nick’s early musical training was as a singer and also as a drummer and world percussionist. He grew up singing in good choirs, and at the same time he was mentored by world-renown percussionist Jamey Haddad from an early age, spent a formative year studying percussion in Brazil, and attended the New School University’s Jazz and Contemporary Music Program in New York City.</p> <p>Nick has performed and recorded internationally as a drummer/percussionist with a wide range of musicians including American musical icon James Taylor, keyboardist-composer Paul Halley (Nick’s father), the late Oscar Castro-Neves (Brazilian guitarist), Chris Norman (Celtic flute), Old Man Luedecke (banjo), Suzie LeBlanc (Baroque and Acadian vocalist), Dinuk Wijeratne (piano), David McGuinness &amp; Revenge of the Folk Singers (Scotland), Concerto Caledonia (Trad – Baroque crossover), Kinan Azmeh (clarinet), Nazih Borish (oud), Joseph Petric (accordion), Roberto Occhipinti (bass), Sandeep Das (tabla), Glenn Patscha (keyboards), and Viradouro Escola de Samba. In 2008, Nick released his debut recording Barnum Hill, featuring Nick’s own compositions performed by Matt Brewer, Adam Niewood, and other young jazz stars in New York City, and produced and engineered by the late, great Tom Bates. Nick has taught workshops in percussion, music theory, and rhythm at Boxwood Festival, Aldeburgh Festival, Interlochen Summer Festival, Lunenburg Academy of Music Performance (LAMP), and in other venues around the world.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><img class='alignnone size-full wp-image-242419' src='https://www.kingstheatre.ca/kingweb/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/DA-2021-Logo.png' alt='' width='266' height='145' /></p> <div id='i8e4vmc5' class='BaOVQ8 tz5f0K i8e4vmc5 wixui-rich-text' data-testid='richTextElement'> <p><strong>TICKETS:</strong><strong>  </strong></p> <ul> <li><strong>Adults: $40</strong></li> <li><strong>Youth: $10</strong></li> <li>HST Additional</li> </ul> </div> <div class='style__notes___25v7J'> <div class='main-title'> <div class='TitleBlock__TitleMetaDataContainer-sc-1nlhx7j-2 hWHMKr'> <ul> <li><em>Doors open 45 minutes before showtime.</em></li> <li><em>King’s Theatre is committed to accessibility. If price is a barrier to attending, please request a ‘Bursary Ticket’ at the Box Office.</em></li> <li><em>Tickets are not refundable for cash, but can be exchanged for credit with a minimum of 48 hours notice. Credits are valid for 12 months.</em></li> <li><em>All persons require a ticket; we do not allow babes in arms. Children under 12 must be accompanied by a guardian.</em></li> <li><em>Outside food and beverages may not be brought into the theatre.</em></li> <li><em>For more information about Theatre policies, click <a href='https://www.kingstheatre.ca/about/ticket-info/'>here.</a></em></li> </ul> </div> </div> </div> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><iframe title='Ali Baba Dance - Nazih Borish Trio - Roots of Strings: Music of Syria 2023' width='1080' height='608' src='https://www.youtube.com/embed/tGs6PSby6hs?feature=oembed'  allow='accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share' referrerpolicy='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' allowfullscreen></iframe></p> <p><iframe title='Roots of Strings - Nazih Borish Trio - Roots of Strings: Music of Syria 2023' width='1080' height='608' src='https://www.youtube.com/embed/xUOIjuoMDmM?feature=oembed'  allow='accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share' referrerpolicy='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' allowfullscreen></iframe></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> "
      }
    ],
